    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you explain the mechanism of action for ACE inhibitors like Lisinopril? I'm specifically trying to understand how they interact with the Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System (RAAS) to reduce blood pressure.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "ACE inhibitors like Lisinopril work by blocking the enzyme Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE). This enzyme is responsible for converting Angiotensin I into the potent vasoconstrictor, Angiotensin II. By inhibiting this conversion, ACE inhibitors reduce the constriction of blood vessels and decrease the release of aldosterone, ultimately leading to a lowering of blood pressure.",
