    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Got it. So if I'm summarizing for my notes, the main thing is that by stopping the production of Angiotensin II, we're essentially cutting off the signal to the adrenal cortex to produce aldosterone. Does this also impact the sympathetic nervous system or anything else in the RAAS loop that might affect cardiac output?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While the primary direct effect is on aldosterone, reduced Angiotensin II indirectly lowers blood pressure by decreasing systemic vascular resistance, which helps reduce the workload on the heart. Furthermore, Angiotensin II also has direct vasoconstrictive effects, so blocking it lowers this peripheral resistance. This decreased afterload allows the heart to pump more easily, which contributes to reduced cardiac workload and blood pressure.",
      "source": "target",
